  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "Q4.1-001",
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "category": "correctness-source-provenance",
      "artifact": "src/dotnet/Vulgata.Infrastructure/Git/GitWorkingCopyService.cs",
      "location": "AcquireAsync, lines 18-46",
      "affected_requirement": "Story 4.1 requires profiling the Repository identified by its validated Git URL; specs/001-codegraph-extraction/spec.md FR-002 requires cloning or updating that Repository, specs/001-codegraph-extraction/plan.md lines 132-135 require one correct private working copy, and docs/UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md defines Source Anchoring as traceable structural provenance.",
      "evidence": "Repository.UpdateDetails permits GitUrl to change. GitWorkingCopyService derives the reusable checkout solely from Repository.Id; when that directory already contains .git, AcquireAsync checks cleanliness and executes `git pull --ff-only` without verifying that the checkout's configured remote still represents repository.GitUrl. After changing a Repository from remote A to remote B, the next ScanRun therefore continues pulling and profiling A while being persisted under the Repository now configured for B. This materially corrupts source provenance and can retain/profile source from the wrong repository. The pinned tests cover dirty-checkout rejection and initial revision capture but no same-Repository-ID remote-change case.",
      "correction_outcome": "Bind every reusable working copy to the current validated Repository Git URL before pull. If the bound remote identity differs, safely invalidate and recreate the private checkout, or otherwise establish and verify the new remote before acquiring the profiled revision. Add a regression integration test using two distinct local remotes and one Repository ID: acquire A, update GitUrl to B, acquire again, and prove the returned working copy and ProfiledRevision come from B while diagnostics persist no URL credentials."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "REVISE: the provider parser, bounded cleanup, atomic structural persistence, authorization seams, migration constraints, and final-image qualification evidence were inspected on the pinned candidate. One High source-provenance defect remains: a reusable checkout is not rebound or rejected when the Repository Git URL changes, so later ScanRuns can profile the wrong repository. This is independent of the separate acceptance-review axis."
